            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use more horizontal rows than possible colour sequences across ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "n+1",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " columns. Two rows have identical sequences, and two columns in that sequence have the same colour.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Choose $n+1$ distinct vertical lines and $n^{n+1}+1$ distinct horizontal lines. On each horizontal line, record the colours of its intersections with the vertical lines, in the same column order.\n\nEach of the $n+1$ positions has $n$ possible colours, so there are $n^{n+1}$ possible colour sequences. By the pigeonhole principle, two horizontal lines have identical sequences.\n\nWithin their common sequence, $n+1$ entries use only $n$ colours. By the pigeonhole principle, two entries have the same colour. The intersections of these two columns with the two matching horizontal lines form a rectangle with all four corners the same colour. Therefore, such a rectangle must exist.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
